w\n2021-07-20-yj-140min-200nl-Joselyn-Soto-Baljit-Khakh-BioID-Neur-SAPAP-3-B.raw\n2021-07-20-yj-140min-200nl-Joselyn-Soto-Baljit-Khakh-GFP-Astro-SAPAP-1-B.raw\n2021-07-20-yj-140min-200nl-Joselyn-Soto-Baljit-Khakh-GFP-Astro-SAPAP-3-B.raw\n2021-07-20-yj-140min-200nl-Joselyn-Soto-Baljit-Khakh-GFP-Neuro-SAPAP-3-A.raw\n2021-09-17-yj-140min-200nl-Joselyn-Soto-Baljit-Khakh-SAPAP3-KO-1-B.raw\n2021-09-17-yj-140min-200nl-Joselyn-Soto-Baljit-Khakh-SAPAP3-KO-2-A.raw\n2021-09-17-yj-140min-200nl-Joselyn-Soto-Baljit-Khakh-SAPAP3-KO-3-B.raw\n2021-09-17-yj-140min-200nl-Joselyn-Soto-Baljit-Khakh-SAPAP3-KO-4-A.raw\n2021-09-17-yj-140min-200nl-Joselyn-Soto-Baljit-Khakh-SAPAP3-WT-1-A.raw\n2021-09-17-yj-140min-200nl-Joselyn-Soto-Baljit-Khakh-SAPAP3-WT-2-A.raw\n2021-09-17-yj-140min-200nl-Joselyn-Soto-Baljit-Khakh-SAPAP3-WT-4-A.raw\n2022-07-08-Vijaya-Joselyn-Khakh-KO-BioID-1-140min-200nl-B.raw\n2022-07-08-Vijaya-Joselyn-Khakh-KO-BioID-2-140min-200nl-B.raw\n2022-07-08-Vijaya-Joselyn-Khakh-KO-BioID-3-140min-200nl-B.raw\n2022-07-08-Vijaya-Joselyn-Khakh-KO-GFP-1-140min-200nl-B.raw\n2022-07-08-Vijaya-Joselyn-Khakh-KO-GFP-2-140min-200nl-B.raw\n2022-07-08-Vijaya-Joselyn-Khakh-KO-GFP-3-140min-200nl-B.raw\n2022-07-08-Vijaya-Joselyn-Khakh-WT-BioID-1-140min-200nl-B.raw\n2022-07-08-Vijaya-Joselyn-Khakh-WT-BioID-2-140min-200nl-B.raw\n2022-07-08-Vijaya-Joselyn-Khakh-WT-BioID-3-140min-200nl-B.raw\n2022-07-08-Vijaya-Joselyn-Khakh-WT-GFP-1-140min-200nl-B.raw\n2022-07-08-Vijaya-Joselyn-Khakh-WT-GFP-2-140min-200nl-B.raw\n2022-07-08-Vijaya-Joselyn-Khakh-WT-GFP-3-140min-200nl-B.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nAstrocyte and neuron subcompartment proteomes reveal distinct cellular and molecular mechanisms of OCD\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nAstrocytes and neurons coexist and interact in the CNS1,2. Given that many signaling and pathological events are protein-driven, identifying astrocyte and neuron proteomes is essential for elucidating the complex protein networks that dictate their respective contributions to physiology and disease. Here, we used cell- and subcompartment-specific proximity-dependent biotinylation3 to study the proteomes of striatal astrocytes and medium spiny neurons (MSNs) in vivo. We evaluated cytosolic and plasma membrane compartments for astrocytes and MSNs, revealing how these cells differ at the protein level and in their core signaling machinery. We assessed subcellular compartments of astrocytes including end feet and processes to reveal the molecular basis of essential astrocyte signaling and homeostatic functions. Unexpectedly, SAPAP3 proteins (gene; Dlgap3) associated with obsessive compulsive disorder (OCD) and repetitive behaviors4-11 were detected at equivalent levels in striatal astrocyte and MSN plasma membrane and cytosolic compartments. Astrocytic expression was confirmed by RNA-seq, fluorescence in situ hybridization and immunohistochemistry. Furthermore, genetic rescue experiments combined with behavioral analyses and proteomics in a mouse model4 of OCD lacking SAPAP3 revealed contributions of SAPAP3 in astrocytes and MSNs to repetitive and anxiety-related OCD behaviors. Our data define how astrocytes and neurons differ at the protein level and in their major signaling pathways, how astrocyte proteomes vary between physiological subcompartments, and how specific astrocyte and neuronal molecular mechanisms contribute to a psychiatric disease. Targeting both astrocytes and neurons together is likely to be therapeutically effective in complex CNS disorders.\n\n[METHODS]\nProtein samples were subjected to reduction using 5 mM Tris (2-carboxyethyl) phosphine for 30 min, alkylated by 10 mM iodoacetamide for another 30 min, and then digested sequentially with Lys-C and trypsin at a 1:100 protease-to-peptide ratio for 4 and 12 hours, respectively. The digestion reaction was terminated by the addition of formic acid to 5% (vol/vol) with centrifugation. Each sample was then desalted with C18 tips (Thermo Scientific, 87784) and dried in a SpeedVac vacuum concentrator. The peptide pellet was reconstituted in 5% formic acid before analysis by liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry (LC-MS/MS).\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHepatitis c virus subtype 1a; Cicer arietinum (chickpea) (garbanzo); Nicotiana tabacum (common tobacco); Heliocidaris erythrogramma (sea urchin); Merluccius bilinearis (silver hake); Cercopithecus aethiops (green monkey) (grivet); Bos taurus (bovine); Dictyostelium discoideum (slime mold); Brassica oleracea var. botrytis (cauliflower); Apis mellifera (honeybee); Halobacterium salinarum; Gallus gallus (chicken); Macaca mulatta (rhesus macaque); Helicobacter pylori j99 (campylobacter pylori j99); Arabidopsis thaliana (mouse-ear cress); Bombyx mori (silk moth); Equus caballus (horse); Caenorhabditis elegans; Mus musculus (mouse); Arachis hypogaea (peanut); Hordeum vulgare (barley); Enterococcus faecalis (streptococcus faecalis); Drosophila melanogaster (fruit fly); Gadus morhua (atlantic cod); Ictalurus punctatus (channel catfish); Danio rerio (zebrafish) (brachydanio rerio); Candida albicans (yeast); Escherichia coli; Mesocricetus auratus (golden hamster); Brassica napus (rape); Homo sapiens (human); Acyrthosiphon pisum (pea aphid)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
